    <comment>
This file, VK_VIDEO_std_h264.xml, gives the Venus protocol generator
field-level definitions for the StdVideo H.264 decode types.

WHY THIS FILE EXISTS

vk.xml declares these types by name only, because they are defined in the
Khronos video headers rather than in the Vulkan registry:

    &lt;type requires="vk_video/vulkan_video_codec_h264std_decode.h"
          name="StdVideoDecodeH264PictureInfo"/&gt;

With no members, Gen.is_serializable() rejects them, and
_init_supported_types() then filters every struct that points at them out of
the pNext chains. The effect is silent: generation succeeds and the video
commands look complete, but the codec payload never crosses the wire.

Describing the members here lets the existing generator emit field-level
encode/decode/size functions, so there is no hand-written serialization for the
payload structs and no path by which a raw struct copy could creep in.

THE *Flags MEMBERS ARE DELIBERATELY DECLARED, NOT EXPANDED

StdVideoDecodeH264PictureInfoFlags and StdVideoDecodeH264ReferenceInfoFlags are
C bitfields. They are declared here as empty structs and listed in
Gen.BITFIELD_STRUCT_LIST, which routes them to hand-written pack/unpack helpers
instead of member-wise serialization. See templates/types_custom.h and
include/vn_protocol_video_h264_flags.h for why that is necessary: the generated
scalar helpers take pointers, and taking the address of a bitfield is illegal C.
    </comment>

    <types>
        <comment>
Codec enums. vk.xml declares these by name only; declaring them as enums here
is enough for the generator, since is_serializable() accepts the ENUM category
and enums are serialized as their underlying integer.
        </comment>
        <type category="enum" name="StdVideoH264ProfileIdc"/>
        <type category="enum" name="StdVideoH264LevelIdc"/>
        <type category="enum" name="StdVideoH264ChromaFormatIdc"/>
        <type category="enum" name="StdVideoH264PocType"/>

        <comment>
Bitfield flag structs. The `packed` member exists only to satisfy the registry
parser, which requires at least one member; it is never serialized. These types
are listed in Gen.BITFIELD_STRUCT_LIST, which both short-circuits
is_serializable() and excludes them from member-wise emission, routing them to
the explicit shift-based helpers instead so the wire layout does not depend on
implementation-defined bitfield allocation.
        </comment>
        <type category="struct" name="StdVideoDecodeH264PictureInfoFlags">
            <member><type>uint32_t</type> <name>packed</name></member>
        </type>
        <type category="struct" name="StdVideoDecodeH264ReferenceInfoFlags">
            <member><type>uint32_t</type> <name>packed</name></member>
        </type>

        <comment>
Per-picture decode parameters. PicOrderCnt is a fixed two-element array
(STD_VIDEO_DECODE_H264_FIELD_ORDER_COUNT_LIST_SIZE), so its length is part of
the type rather than guest-controlled.
        </comment>
        <type category="struct" name="StdVideoDecodeH264PictureInfo">
            <member><type>StdVideoDecodeH264PictureInfoFlags</type> <name>flags</name></member>
            <member><type>uint8_t</type>  <name>seq_parameter_set_id</name></member>
            <member><type>uint8_t</type>  <name>pic_parameter_set_id</name></member>
            <member><type>uint8_t</type>  <name>reserved1</name></member>
            <member><type>uint8_t</type>  <name>reserved2</name></member>
            <member><type>uint16_t</type> <name>frame_num</name></member>
            <member><type>uint16_t</type> <name>idr_pic_id</name></member>
            <member><type>int32_t</type>  <name>PicOrderCnt</name>[2]</member>
        </type>

        <comment>Per-DPB-slot reference metadata.</comment>
        <type category="struct" name="StdVideoDecodeH264ReferenceInfo">
            <member><type>StdVideoDecodeH264ReferenceInfoFlags</type> <name>flags</name></member>
            <member><type>uint16_t</type> <name>FrameNum</name></member>
            <member><type>uint16_t</type> <name>reserved</name></member>
            <member><type>int32_t</type>  <name>PicOrderCnt</name>[2]</member>
        </type>

        <comment>
Session-parameter types: SPS, PPS and their dependencies.

Every array below has a FIXED extent taken from the STD_VIDEO_H264_* macros, so
a guest cannot describe an oversized list. The only variable-length member is
SPS::pOffsetForRefFrame, whose length is num_ref_frames_in_pic_order_cnt_cycle
(a uint8_t, so bounded at 255 by its own type).
        </comment>
        <type category="enum" name="StdVideoH264AspectRatioIdc"/>
        <type category="enum" name="StdVideoH264WeightedBipredIdc"/>

